{"book":16,"chapter":9,"rubric":"FROM ASSEMBLER TO C / I/O AND DATA / CHAPTER 9","slides":[{"title":"Everything is bytes","sub":"Chapter 9 · representation","short":"Bytes","main":"A type is an agreement on reading bytes; stdint gives exact sizes.","body":[{"t":"scene","labels":["Bytes and type","Sizes","Exact types"],"captions":["Memory has no numbers or structs, only bytes; a type is an agreement on how to read them (a register is just bits too)","sizeof gives a type's size, <limits.h> the ranges: INT_MAX, UCHAR_MAX, LONG_MIN","<stdint.h> has exact-size types: int8_t, uint16_t, int32_t, uint64_t"],"frames":[[{"t":"cell","id":"b0","x":40.0,"y":60.0,"w":54.0,"h":26.0,"text":"0x41","tone":"blue","size":9.5,"sub_size":8.5},{"t":"cell","id":"b1","x":96.0,"y":60.0,"w":54.0,"h":26.0,"text":"0x00","tone":"blue","size":9.5,"sub_size":8.5},{"t":"cell","id":"b2","x":152.0,"y":60.0,"w":54.0,"h":26.0,"text":"0x00","tone":"blue","size":9.5,"sub_size":8.5},{"t":"cell","id":"b3","x":208.0,"y":60.0,"w":54.0,"h":26.0,"text":"0x00","tone":"blue","size":9.5,"sub_size":8.5},{"t":"text","id":"n","x":180.0,"y":112.0,"text":"is it int 65? char 'A'? part of an address?","size":10.0}],[{"t":"code","id":"c","x":20.0,"y":36.0,"lines":["sizeof(char)   // 1","sizeof(int)    // 4","sizeof(long)   // 8","sizeof(void *) // 8"],"lang":"c","size":10.5,"lh":20.0,"w":320.0}],[{"t":"code","id":"c","x":20.0,"y":40.0,"lines":["#include <stdint.h>","uint8_t  a;   // 0…255","int32_t  b;   // −2³¹…2³¹−1","uint64_t c;"],"hl":[1,2,3],"lang":"c","size":10.5,"lh":20.0,"w":320.0}]],"cues":[14.9,23.9]}]},{"title":"Sign and overflow","sub":"Two's complement","short":"Sign","main":"Signed overflow is UB, unsigned wraps around.","body":[{"t":"scene","labels":["Two's complement","Overflow","Comparison"],"captions":["The top bit of a signed number weighs −2ⁿ⁻¹: an 8-bit range is −128…127, asymmetric","Unsigned wraps modulo 2ⁿ (defined); signed overflow is undefined behavior, the compiler may ignore it","Signed vs uns